CHANGED: The hash Clash stores in `clash-manifest.json` to decide whether cached HDL is still valid is now computed with a structural hash over the call graph (`Clash.Core.Subst.hashTerm`) instead of by digesting its `Binary` encoding. Encoding a whole design produced tens of megabytes of bytes that existed only to be hashed; on a large design we measured this step drop from ~1.3s to well under 0.1s. The hash is deliberately not modulo alpha, so a renamed binder still invalidates the cache — Clash splices the occurrence name of a Core binder into the generated HDL verbatim. Manifests written by older versions of Clash will not match, so the first run after upgrading regenerates HDL.
